  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 AM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nHigh pressure centered over northern Lake Michigan early this \nmorning will drift eastward today. The combination of clear \nskies, light winds, and very dry air aloft has allowed temps to \nfall into the 40s for interior locations. Patchy shallow fog has\nalso been observed and should continue through sunrise. While \nsome of this fog could be dense, not expecting any widespread \ncoverage.\n\nA mid-level wave over far southern Saskatchewan will amplify \nwhile tracking ESE across the Upper Great Lakes tonight into \nSunday morning. While mid and upper-level support will be fairly\nrobust, mid-level moisture sufficient for widespread precip \nwill remain confined to near the wave. However, a notable axis \nof steeper lapse rates aloft suggests that a band of isolated to\nwidely scattered mid-level showers could brush the northern CWA\nduring the overnight hours. Have included slight chance showers\nnorth of I-88 during this time. Otherwise, an associated cold \nfront will cross the area with little fanfare during the predawn\nhours through mid-morning Sunday. \n\nBroader WNW flow aloft will prevail across the northern CONUS \nthrough the upcoming week, with the upper-jet displaced into \nsouthern Canada. Meanwhile, persistent high pressure will cut \noff low-level Pacific and Gulf moisture. Cannot rule out \nisolated showers brushing the area to the north on Wednesday and\nagain Thursday night with embedded waves crossing the far \nnorthern Great Lakes, but the area may very well end up precip-\nfree through next week. Temps will be fairly seasonable next \nweek. Monday will likely be the coldest night of the stretch \nwith outlying areas possibly falling into the upper 30s.\n\nKluber",
